  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nCurrently, upper ridging is in place aloft with broad surface high \npressure across the eastern U.S. This will continue the same pattern \nwe've had with mild daytime highs after a cool morning today. With \nslightly warmer temperatures, RH values are likely to drop further \ninto the 30s and upper 20s during the afternoon. The pattern will \nremain similar on Wednesday with ridging and high pressure shifting \nfurther to the east. By Thursday, ridging will be flattened with a \nmore zonal pattern aloft and surface high pressure remaining in \nplace. \n\nBy Friday, a shortwave will eject out of the Rockies with downstream \nridging increasing in our region again. With continued high \npressure, similarly dry and mild conditions are expected during the \nday after a cool morning. For Halloween, the shortwave will approach \nfrom the west with surface high pressure strengthening to our east. \nThis will lead to rain chances approaching from the west, but most \nof the area still looks dry for the night. Depending on evolution \nand the MSLP gradient, breezy conditions are possible in the \nmountains. Otherwise, the best chances for rain will be towards \nSunday, but overall rain totals will be light.",
